Compilation Steps in C 
----------------------

Compilation in C is the process of converting a high-level C program into a machine-executable binary. 

This process happens in multiple well-defined stages, each with a specific responsiblity. 


Overall Flow : 

source.c

	-> preprocessor 
	-> compiler 
	-> assembler 
	-> linker 
	-> executable file 

1. Preprocessing 

	Handled by : C preprocessor 
	Input file : .c 
	Ouput file : .i

	The preprocessor performs text-level processing before actual compilation begins. 

	Main tasks of preprocessing: 

		1. Macro expansion 
		2. Header file inclusion 
		3. Conditional compilation 
		4. Comment removal 
		5. Line continuation handling 

1.1 Macro Expansion 

	Macros defined using #define are replaced with their values. 

	Example : 
		
	#define PI 3.14
	area = PI*r*r;

	After preprocessing : 
	area = 3.14*r*r;

	Note: 
		no type checking is done 
		pure text substitution 

1.2 Header File Inclusion 

	All header files included using #include are copied into the source file. 

	Example : 
	
	#include <stdio.h>

	Internally expands to function declarations such as : 
	int printf(const char *, ...);

	Important : 

		Header files are not compiled seperately 
		They only provide declarations 

1.3 Conditional  Compilation 

	Used to include or exclude code based on conditions 

	Example : 
	
	#ifdef DEBUG 
	printf("Debug mode");
	#endif 

1.4 Comment Removal 

	Both types of comments are removed : 
	
		* Single-line comments (//)
		* Multi-line comments (/**/)

Preprocessing command 

	gcc -E p1.c -o p1.i 

2. Compilation

	Handled by : Compiler 
	Input file : .i
	Ouput file : Assembly code (.s)
	
	The compiler converts preprocessed code into assembly language. 
	The compilation stage is internally divided into multiple phases. 

2.1 Lexical Analysis 

	Also called : scanner 
	
	Function : 
		
		Converts source code into tokens 
	
	Example code : 
	
		int a = 10; 

	Tokens Generated : 
	
		<int> <identifier(a)> <=> <constant(10)> <;>

	Types of tokens : 
		
			keywords (int, return)
			identifiers (a, sum)
			constants (10, 3.14)
			operators (+, -, =)
			special symbols (;,{})

	Error detected : 

		Invalid characters 

2.2 Syntax Analysis 

	Also called : parser 
	
	Function : 

		Checks grammatical structure of the program 
		Builds parse tree or syntax tree 
	
	Example syntax error : 

	int a = ;

	Error detected : 

	Syntax error (invalid statement structure)
	

2.3 Semantic Analysis
	
	Function : 

		Check meaning and logic of statements 
		
	Checks performed : 
	
		Variable declared before use 
		Type compatablity 
		Function argument matching 
		Return type checking 

	Example : 
	
	int a;
	a = "hello";

	Error : 

	Type mismatch error 

2.4 Intermediate code Generation 

	Function: 

		Converts source code into machine-independent intermediate code 
	
	Example: 

		a = b+c;

	Intermediate code: 
	
		t1 = b+c
		a = t1

	Purpose : 

		Easier optimization 
		Portability across architectures 

2.5 CODE OPTIMIZATION

	Function:

		Improves performance and memory usage
		Does NOT change program output

	Types of optimizations:

		Constant folding
		Dead code elimination
		Loop optimization
		Common subexpression elimination

		Example:
		
			int x = 10 * 2;

	Optimized code:
		
		int x = 20;

2.6 CODE GENERATION

	Function:

		Converts optimized intermediate code into assembly language

	Example assembly:
		
		MOV R1, b
		ADD R1, c
		MOV a, R1

	Compilation Command:
		gcc -S program.c

	ASSEMBLING

		Handled by: Assembler (as)
		Input file : .s
		Output file: Object file (.o)

	Functions of assembler:

		Converts assembly code into machine code
		Generates relocation table
		Generates symbol table

	Object file contains:
		
		Machine instructions
		Unresolved external references

		Example:
			printf is NOT resolved here

	Assembler Command:
		gcc -c program.c

	LINKING

		Handled by: Linker (ld)
		Input files : .o files + libraries
		Output file : Executable (a.out / program.exe)

The linker is responsible for combining object files and resolving symbols.

4.1 SYMBOL RESOLUTION (IMPORTANT – printf)

	Example:
		printf("Hello");

	During compilation:

		printf is only declared (from stdio.h)
		Actual definition is in libc library

	During linking:

		Linker searches libc
		Resolves address of printf
		Binds function call to actual implementation

	If library missing:
		
		Undefined reference to printf

4.2 TYPES OF LINKING

	STATIC LINKING:
		
		Library code copied into executable
		Larger file size
		Faster execution
		No external dependency

	Command:
		
		gcc -static program.c

	DYNAMIC LINKING:
	
		Library loaded at runtime (.so files)
		Smaller executable
		Shared among processes

	Default behavior:
		gcc program.c

4.3 LINKER ERRORS

	Common linker errors:

		Undefined reference:

			Function declared but not defined
			Missing library
	
	Multiple definition:
		Same symbol defined in multiple files

	LOADING AND EXECUTION

		Handled by: OS Loader

		Steps:

			Executable loaded into memory
			Memory segments initialized
			Stack and heap created
			Control transferred to main()
			MEMORY LAYOUT OF A C PROGRAM

	Text Segment:

		Contains executable code
		Read-only

	Data Segment:

		Initialized global and static variables

	BSS Segment:

		Uninitialized global and static variables

	Heap:

		Dynamic memory allocation (malloc)

	Stack:

		Function calls
		Local variables
		Return addresses

	IMPORTANT GCC OPTIONS

		-E → Preprocessing only
		-S → Generate assembly code
		-c → Generate object file
		-o → Output file name
		-Wall → Enable warnings

	ERRORS AT DIFFERENT STAGES

		Preprocessing:
		
			Missing header file

		Compilation:

			Syntax errors

		Type errors

			Linking:

				Undefined reference
				Multiple definition

			Runtime:

				Segmentation fault
				Divide by zero





































































































	
		





































































































































 
